[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"React Native: Core Components and UI Elements"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"React Native provides a set of built-in components, also known as core components, that are essential for building user interfaces. These components are platform-agnostic, meaning they render differently on iOS and Android to provide a native look and feel. This tutorial will cover some of the most commonly used core components and UI elements in React Native."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"View"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `View` component is the most fundamental building block for UI in React Native. It's a container that supports layout with Flexbox, style, touch handling, and accessibility controls. It's analogous to a `div` in HTML."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import { View, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <View style={styles.box} />
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    backgroundColor: '#fff',
    alignItems: 'center',
    justifyContent: 'center',
  },
  box: {
    width: 100,
    height: 100,
    backgroundColor: 'red',
  },
});

export default App;"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we create a `View` with a style that centers its content. Inside, we place another `View` styled as a red box.  The `StyleSheet` API is used to define styles, which is a best practice for performance and organization."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Text"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Text` component is used to display text. It supports styling, nesting, and touch handling.  You must wrap any text content within a `Text` component."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import { View, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Hello, React Native!</Text>
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    backgroundColor: '#fff',
    alignItems: 'center',
    justifyContent: 'center',
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 20,
    fontWeight: 'bold',
    color: 'blue',
  },
});

export default App;"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example displays the text "Hello, React Native!" in blue, bold, and with a font size of 20.  The `Text` component's style prop allows you to customize the appearance of the text."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Image"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Image` component is used to display images. It supports local images, remote images (URLs), and base64 encoded images.  You can control the size and scaling of the image using the `style` prop."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import { View, Image, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Image
        source={{ uri: 'https://reactnative.dev/img/tiny_logo.png' }}
        style={styles.image}
      />
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    backgroundColor: '#fff',
    alignItems: 'center',
    justifyContent: 'center',
  },
  image: {
    width: 200,
    height: 200,
  },
});

export default App;"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example displays the React Native logo from a remote URL. The `source` prop is an object with a `uri` key specifying the image URL. The `style` prop sets the width and height of the image."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"TextInput"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `TextInput` component allows users to enter text. It supports various properties for controlling input type, keyboard type, placeholder text, and handling text changes."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { View, TextInput, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  const [text, setText] = useState('');

  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <TextInput
        style={styles.input}
        placeholder="Enter text here"
        onChangeText={newText => setText(newText)}
        defaultValue={text}
      />
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    backgroundColor: '#fff',
    alignItems: 'center',
    justifyContent: 'center',
  },
  input: {
    height: 40,
    width: 200,
    borderColor: 'gray',
    borderWidth: 1,
    padding: 10,
  },
});

export default App;"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example creates a `TextInput` field with a placeholder. The `onChangeText` prop is a function that's called every time the text changes. We use the `useState` hook to manage the text input's value and update it whenever the user types."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"ScrollView"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `ScrollView` component enables scrolling of content that exceeds the screen's boundaries. It's useful for displaying long lists or content that doesn't fit on the screen at once."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import { ScrollView, View, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <ScrollView style={styles.container}>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Item 1</Text>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Item 2</Text>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Item 3</Text>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Item 4</Text>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Item 5</Text>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Item 6</Text>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Item 7</Text>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Item 8</Text>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Item 9</Text>
      <Text style={styles.text}>Item 10</Text>
      {/* Add more items as needed */}
    </ScrollView>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    backgroundColor: '#fff',
    padding: 20,
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 20,
    marginBottom: 20,
  },
});

export default App;"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example displays a `ScrollView` containing a list of `Text` components. If the content exceeds the screen height, the user can scroll to view the remaining items. The `padding` style on the container adds space around the content."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Button"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Button` component is a simple way to create a clickable button. It's platform-specific, meaning it will render as a native button on both iOS and Android.  For more customization, consider using `TouchableOpacity` or `Pressable`."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import { View, Button, StyleSheet, Alert } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  const handlePress = () => {
    Alert.alert('Button pressed!');
  };

  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Button
        title="Press me"
        onPress={handlePress}
      />
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    backgroundColor: '#fff',
    alignItems: 'center',
    justifyContent: 'center',
  },
});

export default App;"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example creates a button with the title "Press me". When pressed, the `onPress` function is called, which displays an alert message. The `Alert` API is used to show a simple alert dialog."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"TouchableOpacity"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `TouchableOpacity` component provides a way to make views respond to touches. When the wrapped view is pressed, it becomes slightly transparent, providing visual feedback to the user. It's more customizable than the `Button` component."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import { View, Text, TouchableOpacity, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  const handlePress = () => {
    alert('TouchableOpacity pressed!');
  };

  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <TouchableOpacity style={styles.button} onPress={handlePress}>
        <Text style={styles.buttonText}>Pressable Button</Text>
      </TouchableOpacity>
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    backgroundColor: '#fff',
    alignItems: 'center',
    justifyContent: 'center',
  },
  button: {
    backgroundColor: 'lightblue',
    padding: 10,
    borderRadius: 5,
  },
  buttonText: {
    fontSize: 16,
  },
});

export default App;"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example creates a custom button using `TouchableOpacity`.  The `TouchableOpacity` wraps a `View` containing a `Text` component. When the `TouchableOpacity` is pressed, the opacity of the button decreases slightly, and the `handlePress` function is called."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"FlatList"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `FlatList` component is used to display a scrolling list of data. It's optimized for performance and is ideal for rendering large lists of items. It only renders items that are currently visible on the screen, improving performance."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import { FlatList, View, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const data = [
  { id: '1', title: 'Item 1' },
  { id: '2', title: 'Item 2' },
  { id: '3', title: 'Item 3' },
  { id: '4', title: 'Item 4' },
  { id: '5', title: 'Item 5' },
];

const App = () => {
  const renderItem = ({ item }) => (
    <View style={styles.item}>
      <Text style={styles.title}>{item.title}</Text>
    </View>
  );

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={data}
      renderItem={renderItem}
      keyExtractor={item => item.id}
    />
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  item: {
    backgroundColor: '#f9c2ff',
    padding: 20,
    marginVertical: 8,
    marginHorizontal: 16,
  },
  title: {
    fontSize: 32,
  },
});

export default App;"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example displays a `FlatList` populated with sample data. The `data` prop is an array of objects, each with an `id` and `title`. The `renderItem` prop is a function that renders each item in the list. The `keyExtractor` prop is used to provide a unique key for each item, which is essential for performance."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"These are just a few of the core components and UI elements available in React Native. By combining these components and leveraging styling, you can create complex and engaging user interfaces. Remember to consult the React Native documentation for a complete list of components and their properties."}]
